X:1
T:Sakura
T:Cherry blossoms: Japanese koto song
C:traditional (Japan)
O:Japan
R:koto song
S:Daisyfield.com transcription (Tom Potter, 2008, donated to the public domain) (koto version; the source book is not named on the page)
N:The cherry-blossom song, played on the koto since the Edo period and taught in Japanese schools since the Meiji era. Words in romaji; they mean: "Cherry blossoms, cherry blossoms! Across the spring sky, as far as you can see: is it mist, or cloud? Their scent rises. Come, come, let us go and see!" Slow and even, like a koto's plucked strings.
N:This koto version sits a fifth below the usual school key, in the in scale on A (A, B flat, D, E, F). Its last two bars (G A B flat, then e d B flat A) differ from the school version, and the book it was taken from is not named, so check them against the version your class knows. The drone on A and E is ours.
M:4/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=72
K:Dm
